Historical record. Paths, versions, measurements and instructions below describe that work, not the current application.


# Dependency refresh keeps one Effect runtime

Effect and its platform, SQL, and test adapters stay at `4.0.0`.
Foldkit still requires that runtime family.
Node stays on version 24, with `@types/node` at `24.19.2`.

## Compatibility evidence

- Shiki `4.5.0` preserves the captured highlighting artifact for all 134 fence jobs.
- That artifact covers 64 configured snippets and 66 ordinary rendered fences.
- htmlparser2 `12.0.0` preserves reader bodies and metadata for all 146 generated pages.
- PGlite `0.5.10` passes the database model and migration tests with fresh, in-memory databases.
- Pi `1.3.0` requires a Promise from the project-status command handler. The handler is now async.
- Both Pi extensions pass a dedicated strict SDK typecheck. The command also passes a Promise and notification smoke check.

PGlite re-resolution separated the Alchemy peer contexts for auth and its adapter.
Auth now declares the existing `drizzle-kit` pin as a development dependency.
Both resolve the same Alchemy instance. No runtime dependency or auth type workaround was added.

## Held updates

| Package | Kept | Reviewed target | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Satori | `0.33.5` | `0.62.0` | The target replaces Yoga with Taffy and changes layout defaults. Asset-generation tests do not establish visual parity. |
| Oxfmt | `0.73.0` | `0.74.0` | Version 0.74 appeared during the run. The owner kept the brief's fixed version set and deferred it to the next sweep. |

Use a separate visual review for Satori.

## Better Auth preserves the existing schema

Better Auth moves from `1.6.2` to `1.7.11`.
Its core, adapters, and telemetry packages resolve to `1.7.11`; utils moves from `0.4.0` to `0.5.0`.
Kysely moves from `0.28.17` to `0.29.4`, which satisfies the Alchemy auth adapter's peer range.
The workspace override keeps auth and its adapters on one Kysely version.

The [1.7.3 release](https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/releases/tag/v1.7.3) restores the 1.6 account schema.
The issuer column exists only in 1.7.0 through 1.7.2.
This upgrade skips those versions and requires no account backfill.

### Schema proof

The proof uses `getSchema` and `getMigrations` from Better Auth, as Alchemy's migration layer does.
Both versions receive the cartridge's `authOptions`, including its device and feedback storage plugins.
D1 uses the native D1 dialect through a local SQLite facade.
Postgres uses the native Postgres dialect through a local PGlite pool facade.
These checks establish local schema compatibility, not deployed database health.

| Vendor | SQL diff from 1.6.2 to 1.7.11 | Upgrade plan against the 1.6.2 schema |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D1 | Empty; both outputs contain 1,737 bytes | No tables, columns, or indexes added |
| Postgres | Empty; both outputs contain 1,998 bytes | No tables, columns, or indexes added |

The schema metadata adds empty `indexes` arrays on all six tables. No field changes.
The cartridge keeps column-level uniqueness for `deviceCode` and `userCode`.
Its explicit empty table-index list avoids duplicate indexes over those existing constraints.
The [schema evidence](../../data/better-auth-1711.json) holds both SQL outputs and their hashes.

### Breaking-change review

The review covers [1.7.0](https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/releases/tag/v1.7.0) through [1.7.11](https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/releases/tag/v1.7.11).
Only 1.7.0 labels breaking changes; later releases include fixes and the account-schema reversal.

| Change | Effect on this cartridge |
| --- | --- |
| Joins move to `advanced.database.joins` | No joins option is configured. |
| Account issuer and identity backfill | Reverted in 1.7.3. SQL remains unchanged. |
| Captcha full paths and wildcards | No captcha plugin. `disabledPaths` is a separate option and stays unchanged. |
| MCP plugin moves to `@better-auth/mcp` | No Better Auth MCP plugin. Our MCP projection belongs to the capability package. |
| OIDC back-channel logout | No OAuth provider plugin or connected OAuth clients. |
| Protected resources and required MCP resource | No OAuth provider, CIMD, or Better Auth MCP plugin. |
| SCIM decoupling, connections, and reprovisioning | No SCIM, SSO, or organization plugin. |
| Two-factor response discriminator | No two-factor plugin. |
| Dynamic origin trusts `Host` | Production supplies the fixed `https://ratstack.sh` base URL. No proxy-header opt-in is needed. |
| Unique device-code lookups | Already enforced by the cartridge's two column-level unique constraints. Keep them. |
| Device codes must be claimed before approval or denial | The approval page first calls authenticated `GET /auth/device?user_code=...`. Existing behavior tests cover rejection before claiming and approval after claiming. |
| Electron PKCE and custom schemes | No Electron integration. |
| Microsoft account identity uses `oid` | No Microsoft provider. |
| Google One Tap requires a client ID | No One Tap plugin or Google provider. |
| Deprecated `oidcProvider` removed | Not used. |
| Generic OAuth becomes a social provider | No generic OAuth or social provider. |
| OAuth device grant moves to `oauthDeviceAuthorization` | We use standalone `deviceAuthorization`, without OAuth grant integration or resource fields. |
| Shared ID-token verifier | No custom upstream provider, PayPal sign-in, or ID-token sign-in. |
| OAuth client registration and metadata | No OAuth provider, CIMD, or Better Auth MCP plugin. |
| OAuth `max_age`, reserved claims, grant authentication, resources, and errors | No OAuth authorization server or custom claim hooks. |
| SSO certificates, validation source, SAML responses, and ACS path | No SSO plugin. |
| Expo async secure storage | No Expo integration. |
| Stripe cancellation callback requires `event` | No Stripe plugin. |
| Auth CLI `grantedScopes` array | The `auth` CLI is not installed or used. |
| API-key atomic adapter operations | No API-key plugin, custom auth adapter, or secondary storage. |

Later releases enable initialization-time schema validation and fix D1 behavior with Kysely 0.29.
The old-schema runtime check exercises signup, sign-in, session cookies, device-code issuance, claiming, and approval with schema validation enabled.
Magic Link and OAuth Proxy require coordinated upgrades in 1.7.7; neither plugin is configured here.
The 1.7.6 password-length check applies to email/password authentication and needs no configuration change.
The [device ownership change](https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/pull/9995) requires claiming before approval or denial.

### Bundle budget

The rebuilt 1.6.2 auth Worker contains 2,418,776 bytes of JavaScript.
The first 1.7.11 build with aligned Kysely contains 2,526,940 bytes and exceeds the unchanged 2,500,000-byte budget.
The auth build adds Vite to Alchemy's existing pure-call annotation list.
This removes unused initializer code from the bundled build-tool dependency graph.
The resulting auth Worker contains 2,358,811 bytes, with 141,189 bytes of headroom.
The public Worker moves from 2,176,768 to 2,176,968 bytes.
Both final measurements include the approval script's new claim request.
No budget is raised, and no schema validation is disabled.

### Consumer steps

1. Take the auth manifest, workspace policy, lockfile, device schema, approval script, and auth build option together.
2. Refresh sources with `pnpm vendor:agent-sources`. Better Auth now uses its available `v1.7.11` tag.
3. Run a non-optimistic frozen install and the full gate.
4. Keep the existing D1 and Postgres auth tables. This configuration needs no migration or backfill.
   - The identical schema holds only for this plugin set: email and password plus device authorization.
   - With the `jwt` and `oauth-provider` plugins, 1.7.11 adds three tables: `oauthResource`, `oauthClientResource` and `oauthClientAssertion`.
   - It also adds 24 columns and 4 indexes, and removes `validAudiences`.
   - Generate the schema for your own plugins, diff it against production, and apply that migration before you deploy.
5. Review the upgrade guide separately if your app adds any plugin listed as unused above.
6. Check the actual target database before a separately approved deployment. This packet deploys nothing.

## The fence stays strict

- The compiler diagnostic severity map is unchanged.
- Ultracite `8.0.1` adds `typescript/no-generated-empty-object-type` at error severity.
- Its broader test-extension exemptions are not adopted.
- Explicit overrides keep `no-empty-function` and `promise/prefer-await-to-then` at error severity for the newly exempted extensions.
- Those extensions are `.mts`, `.cts`, `.mjs`, and `.cjs`.
- Oxfmt `0.73.0` produces no formatting-only changes when `pnpm fix` runs.
- Worker size limits remain 900,000 bytes per entry and 2,500,000 bytes for total JavaScript.

The compiler plugin `0.54.0` adds advisory diagnostics without changing the gate.
The owner approved leaving these suggestions for a gardener pass.

| Newly observed rule | Unique package/file/line locations |
| --- | ---: |
| `schemaClassMissingBrand` | 111 |
| `errorPayloadShadowsName` | 6 |
| `flatMapToTap` | 1 |

`preferTypedSchemaDecoder` already reports 62 unique locations before the update.
Repeated build and test logs must not be counted as separate source findings.

## Consumer checks

1. Take each dependency family with its lockfile changes.
2. Keep all workspace occurrences of Node types and Vitest aligned.
3. Keep auth's development-only `drizzle-kit` pin with the PGlite update.
4. Run a non-optimistic frozen install, then `pnpm turbo run check test build`.
5. Refresh missing source mirrors with `pnpm vendor:agent-sources`.
6. Keep production deployment outside this maintenance packet.

The [source mirror migration note](./xstate-alpha69-migration.svx) documents the held XState family.
The [code snippets system](../../resources/lore/code-snippets.svx) owns the highlighting boundary.
